            <ArticleTitle>Transplantation sites for porcine islets.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1972-1976</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00125-017-4363-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS/HYPOTHESIS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Xenotransplantation has great potential to provide beta cell replacement and thereby provide a cure for large numbers of people with type 1 diabetes. Crucial to the success of xenotransplantation is establishment of the most viable sites for transplantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We compared porcine islet tissue transplanted into kidney, liver and spleen in pig recipients as assessed by blood glucose levels and IVGTT.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Kidney was the superior site for porcine islet tissue transplantation, followed by liver then spleen. This was demonstrated by IVGTTs showing significant difference between the peak glucose levels: 22.8 ± 2.9 mmol/l for kidney compared with 26.8 ± 1.3 mmol/l for spleen and 24.7 ± 1.7 mmol/l for liver.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS/INTERPRETATION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Kidney grafts are not as feasible in humans and liver results were relatively poorer than spleen. For islet transplantation to be viable and successful in the longer term, there remains a need for future investigation of alternative sites.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
